\section{Find the Program Point Where the Fact Changes}

SSA gives each name one definition, but it does not give every fact about that name one definition. A particular pointer value can be known nonnull in one branch and unknown after a join. The same abstract resource can go from open to closed as statements execute, with no corresponding change to the SSA name that refers to it. For a memory or typestate property, treating every def-use edge as a complete description of how the fact flows would miss these program points.

Khedker, Sanyal, and Karkare's \emph{Data Flow Analysis: Theory and Practice} develops the more general formulation: an abstract domain, a merge operation, boundary facts, and transfer functions. A CFG solver stores a fact at each relevant point, applies block or edge transfers, and visits dependents when facts change. Monotone transfers on a finite-height domain allow a worklist to converge; a domain with long or infinite ascending chains may require widening or another termination strategy. A may analysis often merges sets by union; a must analysis typically intersects facts guaranteed by all incoming paths. These are semantic choices, not differences in how SSA names are assigned.

\section{Represent a Branch Fact as a Name}

Navindra Umanee's \emph{Shimple: An Investigation of Static Single Assignment Form} compares extended SSA (eSSA) with Static Single Information (SSI) form and combines features of both in Extended Shimple. An eSSA-style $\pi$ defines a new name carrying the \emph{same value} as its operand but under a narrower path condition. Consider a procedure with argument \texttt{p} and the following abstract CFG:

\begin{verbatim}
entry: if p != null then yes else no
yes:   pn = pi(p, entry's true edge)
       consumeField(pn)
       goto join
no:    goto join
join:  consumeField(p)
\end{verbatim}

The true edge proves \texttt{pn} nonnull. The first field access does not need another null check if that is the only condition it would check. The operation at \texttt{join} still requires its check: the direct path through \texttt{no} reaches it with a null value. A dense implementation would put the nonnull fact on the edge and intersect guarantees at the join. A sparse implementation propagates the fact from the new name and must not give it a non-dominating use. For a useful negative test, add another entrance to \texttt{yes} that bypasses the null test. The $\pi$ fact belongs on the tested edge, not at the shared block entry.

SSI adds $\sigma$ functions that split a name into edge-specific names at forks. These complement $\phi$ functions at joins and can expose dependency edges for backward analyses as well. The representation saves some propagation work when only a few edges change an interesting fact, but it increases the IR's size and makes transformations responsible for maintaining the edge names.

\section{Calls, Returns, and Abstract State}

Across procedures, following each call edge to the callee and permitting a return to any caller gives paths that never occur. For example, two sites call an identity function with arguments \texttt{4} and \texttt{9}. An analysis that allows a return from the first call to arrive at the second call's continuation will consider \texttt{4} a possible result of the second call. That can be a sound overapproximation, but it loses a constant the optimizer could have used. A summary stating that the result equals the argument can be applied separately at both sites. With unknown callees, conservatively combine the possible summaries with any required unknown effects.

Sagiv, Reps, and Horwitz's \emph{Precise Interprocedural Dataflow Analysis with Applications to Constant Propagation} exploits matched call and return paths for distributive environment transformers. It gives exhaustive and demand algorithms for that setting; not every relational or heap analysis has the needed distributivity. The dossier's Chapter~38, \emph{Cross-Program Semantic Summary Exchange}, considers sharing summaries across larger boundaries. A summary there is useful only under compatible semantics, assumptions about calls, and a conservative description of unmodeled effects.

