\section{Project a Call Summary at Each Call Site}

A constant summary can be more useful if it represents a relation
rather than one joined return value. Use a small pure routine with
one integer parameter, no effects, and a summary that returns the
argument plus an offset when a guard holds. The summary is valid for
all arguments in the routine's declared mathematical-integer domain;
its disjoint arms cover that domain. At each call site, specialize
the summary using the local abstract argument. The result must
overapproximate all possible returns at that site's own continuation.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Abstract argument $A$, guarded return cases $S$
\Let{R}{\bot}
\ForAll{$(g,f)\in S$}
  \To{Intersect with the case guard before mapping the value.}
  \Let{B}{\operatorname{restrict}(A,g)}
  \If{$B\ne\bot$}
    \Let{R}{R\sqcup \operatorname{transfer}(f,B)}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\To{Return this fact only to the requesting site.}
\State \Return $R$
\end{sudocode}

Take a routine returning $x+2$ for $x\ge0$, and returning $0$
otherwise. At one call site with $A=[4,4]$, the negative case
has empty input and the result is $[6,6]$. At a separate site
with $A=[-3,-3]$, only the negative case contributes and the
result is $[0,0]$. Joining the arguments before specializing
would give $[-3,4]$ and the coarser answer $[0,6]$ at both
sites. Also test a third call with $A=[-1,2]$: it must keep both
cases and return $[0,4]$. The gap between $0$ and $2$ is an
accepted loss of interval precision, not a mismatched return.

After any prefix of cases, $R$ covers exactly the required
union up to the loss of the chosen abstraction: it covers
every return from input values satisfying one of the processed
guards. The simple implementation scans all cases per site.
It must not reuse a specialized result solely because two calls
share a callee; the input fact also belongs in the cache key.
For a routine with effects or an unresolved indirect call,
this return-only summary is insufficient. Include conservative
effect and exceptional summaries before using it to remove any
call or any check after that call.

\section{Keep a Dense Reference Solver}

For a new sparse analysis, first implement the same transfer rules densely on a small CFG, including exceptional edges and call effects if modeled. Compare projected facts at the points clients query, using the same abstraction and path assumptions. Then measure graph size, transfers evaluated, and effects on optimization decisions. Sparsity changes where and when facts are stored and propagated; an unexplained loss of precision is not a required cost of moving information onto sparse edges.
